11:["$","$L42",null,{"faqItems":[{"q":"What is tonsillitis?","a":"Tonsillitis is inflammation of the tonsils, which are tissues located at the back of the throat. It is commonly caused by viral infections and can also be caused by bacteria. Symptoms may include sore throat, swollen tonsils, painful swallowing, fever and swollen neck glands."},{"q":"What are the most common symptoms of tonsillitis?","a":"Common symptoms include sore throat, red or swollen tonsils, painful swallowing, fever, swollen lymph nodes in the neck, bad breath and sometimes white or yellow patches on the tonsils. Symptoms can vary depending on whether the cause is viral or bacterial."},{"q":"Is tonsillitis caused by bacteria or viruses?","a":"Both viruses and bacteria can cause tonsillitis. Viral infections are common, while bacterial infections such as group A Streptococcus can also cause tonsillitis. A medical evaluation and, when appropriate, testing can help determine the likely cause."},{"q":"How long does tonsillitis usually last?","a":"The duration depends on the underlying cause and the individual's recovery. Many acute infections improve over several days, but persistent, severe or recurrent symptoms should be evaluated by a doctor rather than repeatedly self-treated."},{"q":"When should I see an ENT doctor for tonsillitis?","a":"You should consider an ENT evaluation if tonsillitis keeps returning, symptoms are severe or persistent, swallowing becomes difficult, tonsils remain significantly enlarged, you have frequent tonsil stones or bad breath, or enlarged tonsils are associated with snoring or sleep-related breathing problems."},{"q":"What is recurrent tonsillitis?","a":"Recurrent tonsillitis means repeated episodes of tonsil inflammation over time. The number and severity of episodes, their impact on daily life and whether they are appropriately documented are important when deciding on long-term management."},{"q":"When is tonsillectomy recommended?","a":"Tonsillectomy may be considered for selected patients with recurrent severe tonsillitis, significant obstructive symptoms caused by enlarged tonsils, certain complications or other specific medical indications. The decision should be made after an ENT assessment."},{"q":"Are enlarged tonsils always a reason for surgery?","a":"No. Enlarged tonsils do not automatically require surgery. Treatment depends on symptoms, the degree of enlargement, recurrent infections, swallowing problems, sleep-related breathing symptoms and other clinical findings."},{"q":"Can tonsillitis cause bad breath?","a":"Yes. Bad breath can occur during tonsil infections. Persistent bad breath may also be associated with tonsil stones, chronic tonsil problems, dental conditions or other causes, so persistent symptoms may require evaluation."},{"q":"What are tonsil stones?","a":"Tonsil stones are small deposits that can form in the crypts or pockets of the tonsils when debris, mucus and other material collect and harden. They may cause bad breath, throat irritation or a sensation of something stuck in the throat."},{"q":"Can tonsillitis cause difficulty breathing?","a":"Severe tonsil swelling can sometimes affect the upper airway. Difficulty breathing should be treated as an urgent symptom, particularly if it is sudden, worsening or associated with significant throat swelling."},{"q":"Is tonsillectomy painful?","a":"Tonsillectomy is performed under anaesthesia, so the patient does not experience surgical pain during the procedure. Throat pain is common during recovery and can last for several days. Recovery instructions, hydration, diet and prescribed medicines are important after surgery."}]}]
